  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Far-right hero and thorn in the side of the \nEU, Viktor Orbán just lost big in Hungary.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6080,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yet Hungary's economy is in the drain and Orbán 's \nultra-wealthy friends still control the judiciary  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13040,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the media. So what's next for Hungary? Can \nPéter Magyar purge the illiberal deep state Orbán  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21120,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "left behind? And can he save Hungary's economy? \nOr will he just be like Macron or Trudeau,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27040,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a disappointing reformer? Or perhaps even like \nPutin or Erdogan, a promised liberal reformer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33600,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who is actually just the next strongman. To \nanswer these questions, we need to do a quick  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39920,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "recap of how Orbán lost. Most political analysts \nbelieve that Orbán lost primarily for two reasons.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48080,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, after initially bringing unemployment down \nby a lot after getting elected in 2010, Hungary's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "economy under Orbán has performed quite a bit \nworse than comparable East European economies.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60640,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, Hungarian wages have stayed far \nbehind comparable countries such as, for example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66720,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Poland, but also many other countries in the EU \nsince 2008. What's worse, after a massive COVID  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73600,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spending spree, a rise in energy prices, and the \ndepreciation of the Forint, Hungaria suffered the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79760,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worst inflation crisis in all of Europe under \nOrban. The second reason analysts often mention  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86720,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that the scale of corruption under Orban was \njust getting too much. For example, Orban's rich  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "friends, such as his childhood friend, and I \nhope I pronounce it correctly, Lőrinc Mészáros,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97920,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who just so happens to have become Hungary's \nrichest man since meeting Orban, have won up to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104720,
    "dDurationMs": 8800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "25% of all state contracts, coincidentally, in the \nyears after Orban was elected. But the thing is,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's easy to accept corruption if everyone is \ngetting richer. But if the economy is stagnating,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118800,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if inflation is eating away at people's salaries, \nthen too blatant corruption can really tick off  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124320,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your voters. So ticked off in fact that despite \nspending years to rig the political system in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "his favor by changing voting districts and \ndespite controlling Hungary's entire traditional  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "media landscape like radio and TV, Orban's \ngovernment had just gotten so blatantly bad  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142320,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at his job and so blatantly corrupt that it did \nnot matter anymore. His far-right Fidesz party,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148960,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which used to have a super majority in 2022, now \nlost badly to the conservative Péter Magyar and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157520,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "his Tisza party. The only other party that cleared \nthe 5% voting threshold to get into parliament  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165120,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was the far-right Mi Hazank party. And for us, \nit's just Orban and Peter Maguar. And that's a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173040,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "real problem for Orban because he had manipulated \nthe voting system in such a way that it benefited  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177760,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the biggest party even more than it always did. \nAnd this is how with just 44.87% of the vote.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185520,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Orban got a so-called supermaajority in 2014 which \nallowed them to rewrite the constitution to give  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192000,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both his party more power and to enrich themselves \nand to entrench themselves by creating a massive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199840,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "deep state of Fidesz loyalists which could only be \nremoved by a super majority such as the president  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208000,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who can refer any bill to be approved by the \nconstitutional court which consists of not one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214000,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not two but 15 Fidesz loyalists. The next part of \nthe deep state is the so-called fiscal council,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220880,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which could nuke any new government budget at any \nmoment. Fourth, there is the prosecutor general,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226240,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who can choose to try to prosecute Tisza officials \nwhile protecting Fidesz oligarchs. And finally,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "element number five, there is the media authority, \nwhich ensures the media remains loyal to Orban.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These deep state defenses were why it was \nso important that Magyar not just won,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but rather that he won by a landslide. And \nironically, Hungary's unfair electoral system  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249760,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that benefits the biggest party helped him \nto do it, turning 53% of the vote into the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256480,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two-thirds supermajority that he needs \nto dismantle most of these elements of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261120,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Orbán's deep state. At least in theory. Because \nhonestly, this is also where expectations need to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be realistic. These institutions are not empty \nshells. You're dealing with people, networks,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273120,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and incentives built over 16 years of Orban rule. \nReplacing laws is fast. Changing how the system  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280880,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually behaves is much slower. So, this is a \nturning point, but it's the start of a process,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not the end. On top of that, while Orban probably \ndid not expect to lose by this much, he still left  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293200,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "behind some more insurance by privatizing major \nparts of the state into independent trusts and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300240,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "institutions controlled by Fidesz loyalists \nsuch as the Foundation for Hungarian Culture,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306720,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "several university trusts, and the state oil \ngiant MOL New Europe Foundation. So there's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313280,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the NGO and trust part of his defense, and then \nthere's also the second part of his defense,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320080,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is that Fidesz oligarchs still control some \nof Hungary's biggest corporations, such as the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326960,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "massive MBH bank and 4IG Telecom group. So even \nif Magyar and his two-thirds majority is able to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "purge the official government, he still inherits \na government that is much weaker than the one that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340640,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Orban inherited in 2010. And this is important \nbecause Hungary's economy is a mess. Specifically,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347920,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's still one of Europe's poorest countries with \ndespite Orban spending about 5% of GDP on boosting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355200,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fertility rates. And it seemed to work initially. \nNow fertility has fallen back to its all-time low  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363440,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "levels, far below replacement rates. And combined \nwith Orban's ultra strict immigration policies,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369840,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it means that Hungary's population is steadily \nfalling while those of neighboring countries  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375040,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are rising. Combine this spending with what many \nhave argued was essentially vote buying, and it's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379920,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no surprise that Hungary's government deficit is \none of the worst in the EU. While inflation and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "borrowing costs are some of the highest in the \nEU. So other than cutting corruption, which we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have discussed will be quite difficult. Magar's \ngovernment is simply starting from a very weak  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "position when it comes to the economy. But on the \nbright side, promising to restore the rule of law  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401600,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will gradually unlock about 22 billion euros in EU \nfunding. So, not surprisingly, Hungary's currency  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409120,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Forint has rallied on the news that Péter \nMagyar won. So all in all, to answer our question,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416080,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what's next for Hungary? Their new prime minister, \nPéter Magyar, has a supermajority he needs to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422000,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "begin the difficult process of dismantling \nOrbán's deep state. Yet Orban has built some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427760,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pretty impressive defenses to defend his oligarch \nfriends. And on top of that, a severely weakened  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433600,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hungarian government is borrowing like crazy \nwhile the economy is stagnant and people are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440080,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leaving the country in droves. That's really \nnot an easy situation to come back from. Yet,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when it comes to liberals, especially in the EU, \nthey are hopeful. Hopeful that Magyar has signaled  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that he'd break with Orban's policy of aligning \nhimself with the people who want to see the EU  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gone. Like both Trump and Putin who wanted him to \nwin this election. EU Liberals are hopeful that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hungary will stop blocking Ukraine. Hopeful that \nHungary's democratic backsliding will be reverted,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466400,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the EU will remain a collective of not just \nfree democracies, which Hungary clearly was,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473840,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "given that Orban lost by a landslide, but more so \nthat the EU will now be a collective of free and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482000,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fair democracies, where the media is independent \nfrom the ruling party, and where judges are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488560,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "independent. In summary, they hope the EU will \nagain be a collective of liberal democracies,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495600,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "free plus fair. But whether or not that will \nbe the case will not just be a matter of how  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501680,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "easy Orban's deep state can be dismantled. It will \nnot just be a matter of whether or not the economy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507360,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can be saved. It will also be a matter of what \nPéter Magyar wants. because he can say that he  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wants to stop democratic backsliding, but I think \nit's important to keep in mind that Orban himself  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519760,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "started out as a liberal. Erdogan in Turkey \nstarted out as a pro-EU democracy reformer. Heck,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526800,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even Vladimir Putin started out as a reformer whom \nEU countries were quite hopeful about. Peter Mar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533440,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a staunch conservative that started out as an \nurban ally. So, will the Orban deep state truly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541200,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be dismantled or will he just take it over? Time \nwill tell. And in the meantime, if you want to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dig deeper, I highly recommend that you check out \nthe excellent analysis by our advertising sponsor,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552880,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Economist, which I always check out for the \nresearch process of my videos. For this episode  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559840,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in particular, I highly recommend that you start \nwith their analysis on how opposition movements  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all around the world can learn from Hungary's \nexperience and then follow up with this article  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570640,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about how Orban's government has perfected \nspreading its populist ideology to the wider  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576000,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "world. And then finally, I recommend you check out \ntheir podcast episode on how Hungary's opposition  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581600,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leader Péter Magyar became so successful. I think \nas these examples show, The Economist delivers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589520,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "insight that can help you see the bigger picture \nand think for yourself. This is why I almost  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595760,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "always rely on their analysis for my research and \nit is why I highly recommend that you subscribe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601520,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I'm excited to say that they're offering a special \n35% discount for money and macro viewers right  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608880,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now. And whether you prefer their daily journalism \nin the Economist app or you're like me and enjoy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615360,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "catching up on the global economy over the weekend \nwith a nice cup of coffee and the print edition,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621760,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you'll always stay on top of the latest \nglobal developments and support journalism  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626720,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that values factual information, integrity, and \nan independent point of view. So don't miss out.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634640,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Click the link in the description below \nor head over to economist.com/moneymro"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642000,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to claim your exclusive 35% discount date."
    } ]
  } ]
